We experienced an issue with native Datagrok on restoring layouts containing viewers linked to a different table.

**Steps to reproduce:**
1. add two tables
2. add scatterplot (or any other viewer) to second table
3. in viewer configuration select other table as data source
4. store view layout
5. close table and reopen
6. load previously stored layout

**Expected behaviour:** Layout is restored without any issue
**Actual behaviour:** Layout is restored but red warning messages are shown in grok

<img width="537" alt="Screenshot 2022-02-22 at 16 42 16" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3170680/155167051-facc2d3b-28a4-4492-b537-43387a939e76.png">

I was also able to catch that behaviour with this Grok script:

```javascript
/name: Layout error messages
//description: Hello world script
//language: javascript

const table2 = grok.data.demo.geo();
const table1 = grok.data.demo.demog();

const view2 = grok.shell.addTableView(table2);
const view1 = grok.shell.addTableView(table1);

// add scatterplot for non-active table
view1.scatterPlot({ table: 'Table' });

ui.dialog({title:'Let\'s break the system...' })
	.add(ui.span(['Click "Ok" to re-open view and apply layout.']))
    .onOK(() => {
  		// create layout
		const layout = view1.saveLayout();
        grok.shell.closeTable(table1);
  		const table3 = grok.data.demo.demog();
  	    const view3 = grok.shell.addTableView(table3);
  		view3.loadLayout(layout);
    })
    .show();
```

It seems that Datagrok assumes that viewer without a "table" option in the stored layout is always restored on the current active dataframe as only viewers selecting data from different table have this option in. This behaviour creates issues in case the current active table is not the same as it was when the layout was exported.

**Steps to reproduce (executed in the script)**
1. add "geo" demo table
2. add "demog" demo table
3. add two scatterplots
4. select "demog" table as table option for second table
5. store layout of current table
6. Close both tables
7. Restore both tables but in reversed order ("demog" first before "geo" table)
8. Apply layout

**Expected behavior:**
- layout is restored as it was previously

**Actual behavior**
- second Scatterplot is bound to active table and is different than before

**Solution proposal**
Can we add this table option to ALL VIEWERS by default so that the selected table is always stored with the viewer in the layout. This way Grok would always have a reference to the correct table no-matter the "table order"?

**Script:**
```
//name: Layout restore on different table order
//description: Hello world script
//language: javascript

const table1 = grok.data.demo.demog();
const table2 = grok.data.demo.geo();

const view1 = grok.shell.addTableView(table1);
const view2 = grok.shell.addTableView(table2);

// add scatterplot for non-active table
view2.scatterPlot({ table: 'demog 10000' });
view2.scatterPlot();

ui.dialog({title:'Let\'s break the system...' })
	.add(ui.span(['Click "Ok" to re-open view and apply layout.']))
    .onOK(() => {
  		// create layout
		const layout = view2.saveLayout();
  		console.log(layout);
  
  		// close all tables
        grok.shell.closeTable(table2);
        grok.shell.closeTable(table2);
  
  		// open table in reversed order
  		const table3 = grok.data.demo.demog();
  		const table4 = grok.data.demo.geo();
  
  	    const view4 = grok.shell.addTableView(table4);
    	const view3 = grok.shell.addTableView(table3);
  
  		// apply layout and let it "break"
  		view3.loadLayout(layout);
    })
    .show();

```

